A||||6||Black88GT||Getting a credit report||||5||
Z||000000||Black88GT||09-10-2001||03:58 PM||||Has anyone recently obtained a Credit report for themselves?  Which methods/means did you use?  <P>I went to freecreditreport.com but it seems like a lot of B.S.  There terms are sketchy at best and they want to send you a lot of junk mail it seems.  I would rather pay 10 bucks or so to get a credit report then go through all that B.S.<P>any help is appreciated!||||reg||5
Z||000001||LarryB||09-10-2001||04:34 PM||||There are 3 major credit reporting agencies in the US, Equifax (used to be TRW Credit Data), Experian, and TransUnion. You can contact each of them directly off the 'net to order a copy of their latest report sent to you.  You do *not* need to go through some screening agency to get your report.<P>These are all independent agencies (they're direct competitors to each other) and not every company reports to all of them, so you need to order a credit report from each of them to get the full story. <P>I'm not positive if the law has changed, but it used to be that if you were denied credit you were entitled to a free copy of your credit report from the agency that provided the credit information.<P>My mother worked for TRW Credit Data for several years, she found it an eye-opening experience.   In her opinion firefighters were the nicest people to deal with, cops and doctors were the worst, some doctors were real lowlifes.||||reg||1
Z||000002||mrfiero||09-10-2001||06:53 PM||||I'm about to get a copy of mine.  In Colorado by law we are entitled to one free report per year (woo hoo!)....just move to Denver and save yourself a few bucks!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Larry B is correct about the denied for credit thing......if you apply for a Sears card or any other credit card account and are denied you are entitled to a free report.  The details will be listed on the denial form you get in the mail from Sears or whomever denied you the credit.<P>Or you could just shell out the $10-$20 and order one directly through TRW or one of the other credit reporting agencies.<P>I personally think credit ratings are being entirely overused (and thus overrated) by "the man".  You can't do anything without having your credit checked......I was denied an apartment because I had previous slow credit (hell, you can get denied for a job for crying out loud).  <P>I haven't had or used a credit card in years (got in trouble when I went away to school back in '89), but the past is still haunting me to this day.  I think it's a crock and a half that a few late or missed payments many moons ago can affect your quality of life now.<P>Anyways.....I'll get off my soapbox now (sorry for rambling).  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Black88GT||09-10-2001||07:25 PM||||Hey I found out in Maryland you get a free one too!<P>Only question is, does that mean a free one from each of the "big 3" or is that a free report from just one?  Not sure about that.<P>If I can only get one, is there any particular difference (i.e. is there a "best")?<P><p>[This message has been edited by Black88GT (edited 09-10-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||mrfiero||09-10-2001||08:56 PM||||Cool!  I can't help you on which one though......the letter we get in the mail each year (to get the free report) is from TRW (I think?.....I didn't really look).||||reg||1
Z||000005||LarryB||09-10-2001||09:41 PM||||Equifax (the old TRW Credit Data) used to allow individuals to get 1 free report each year.<P>And they are not all identical; many agencies report to only 1 or 2 of the 3 major bureaus.  Sears reports to all of them, which is why Sears is a good card to have if you want to build up good credit, but bad if you want to stiff someone. <P>Mike, it sounds like you should pull your credit reports as well.  The bureaus are notorious for not properly maintaining individual credit histories, so you may have information that although valid was never removed after the retention period had expired, it happens all the time.  If it exists you can get it removed by requesting t in writing. (For example, monthly credit history on a normal credit card is only supposed to be retained for 12 months.)<P>You can also dispute any legally valid (note that 'legally valid' doesn't necessarily equal 'accurate') information in your history by including a letter detailing the dispute and it will become part of your credit history, although according to my mother the letters generally carried little weight with the potential creditors.<P>One thing to keep in mind:  the credit agencies do NOT make the decisions whether or not you are extended credit. Their ONLY function is to provide the information given them by your creditors.  The decisions to grant or deny credit is made SOLELY by the agency you applied to for credit. If you are denied credit, you want to yell at the loan officer where you applied, **not** the person at the phone at the credit agency.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||artherd||09-10-2001||10:25 PM||||Fight Club.  That's all I have to say.<P>The whole 'established credit' thing is backwards, I have a newly founded small buisness and I can't get crap.  Because I need it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">    One day if I'm rich and fameous I'll be able to borrow all the money on the face of the earth, plus take out options on Mars, but by then, I won't NEED it!!  aargh!!  (ok, so I lied and said more  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
